  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 257 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The dry and seasonable stretch continues through \ntonight. Highs will be in the 70s to low 80s and lows will be in the \n50s and low 60s. An occluding low will track well to the north \nTuesday into Wednesday, bringing several rounds of showers. The \nshowers look to begin during the day Tuesday and continue into \nWednesday afternoon. Right now, the severe threat is relatively low. \nThere does not look to be a well-defined boundary, instead several \nrounds of showers with plenty of clouds look to move through. There \nis very little time for solar heating and instability to form, and \nthe airmass will be relatively cool to start with. With the low \ntracking well to the north, the best synoptic forcing will be well \nto the north, though there will be adequate wind shear. Overall, the \nregion is in an SPC marginal risk which seems representative. While \nan isolated strong to severe storm is possible, that looks to be the \nextent of it. The system does not look to provide much of a flooding \nthreat either. GEFS/EPS/CMCE combined ensemble probabilities of \nreceiving more than an inch of rain are between 10-30 percent. While \nthere will be areas of convective enhancement, the storms will be \nquick-moving and there will be gaps between the showers. PWATs \nshould rise into the 1.5-1.75 inch range but with the expected \nsetup, that should not pose too much of a problem.  While limited \nwildfire smoke may return for Tuesday, its concentration will be \nsignificantly less than on Saturday and it will likely not have much \nof an effect on the convection.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Thursday night through Sunday will feature quieter \nweather as we close out the week. Upper level trough will pull away \nfrom the region and then surface high pressure will ridge over our \nregion Thursday night. A low pressure system passes well to our \nnorth Friday into Friday night, our region should not have any \nprecipitation associated with this system. The weekend looks mainly \ndry with near normal temperatures. Next chance for precipitation \nwill be Sunday night into Monday night as a frontal system and upper \nlevel trough crosses the region.",
